<td id="S3.T1.10.12.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S3.T1.10.12.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">✓</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.10.12.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span id="S3.T1.10.12.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Feb 19, 2026</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.10.12.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span id="S3.T1.10.12.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">with/without custom tools</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.10.13" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.10.13.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T1.10.13.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">GLM</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.10.13.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T1.10.13.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">GLM-5</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.10.13.3" class="ltx_td ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"></td>
<td id="S3.T1.10.13.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T1.10.13.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">✓</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.10.13.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T1.10.13.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Feb 11, 2026</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.10.13.6" class="ltx_td ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Scaffolds.</span>  <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">EVMbench</span> generally pairs each model with its vendor’s scaffold (e.g., Claude with Claude Code, GPT with Codex CLI), with one cross-scaffold test (<span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GPT-5.2</span> on OpenCode). The authors note that “tooling and workflow choices materially affect outcomes,” yet this single comparison is not enough to systematically separate scaffold effects from model effects. To disentangle the two, we run three models (<span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Claude Opus 4.5</span>, <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Claude Sonnet 4.5</span>, and <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GPT-5.3-Codex</span>) across all three scaffolds listed in Table <a href="#S3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 3.1. Agent Configurations ‣ 3. Evaluation Setup ‣ Re-Evaluating EVMBench: Are AI Agents Ready for Smart Contract Security?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>: Claude Code, Codex CLI, and OpenCode. Other models either could not run reliably on OpenCode or lacked native scaffold support. Table <a href="#S3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 3.1. Agent Configurations ‣ 3. Evaluation Setup ‣ Re-Evaluating EVMBench: Are AI Agents Ready for Smart Contract Security?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> lists the three scaffolds, which cover both vendor-provided and open-source options.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T2" class="ltx_table">

<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2. </span>Scaffolds used in our evaluation.</figcaption><div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
